course: everyday
lesson: 345
title: When someone else takes your punishment
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Letting someone take your punishment is like stealing from them: you take their good name and their free time. Finn in Cork drew with chalk on the school wall, and when the teacher asked, his friend Rory got the blame and said nothing. Rory missed football practice as a punishment, and kept looking at Finn. Finn felt sick inside all week.
explain: On Friday Finn told the teacher it was him, said sorry to Rory and scrubbed the wall clean himself. Rory was cross for a day, then said, «Took you long enough», and they walked home together.
explain: Finn's late mornings went another way. Twice he said «the bus was late» when he had dawdled, and twice nobody checked. He began to think the excuse always worked. The third time his teacher had watched him stroll past the window. He was marked late and the excuse never worked again — small excuses add up to a big habit.
explain: Worst of all was the secret. Finn told a friend's secret and then blamed his best friend for it, scared of losing the others. The whole group fell out, and his best friend wouldn't speak to him. Only owning up — «It was me, I'm sorry» — saved the friendship.
fact: Letting someone else take your punishment takes away their good name and their time.
fact: Silence can be a lie when it lets someone innocent be punished.
fact: When two children fight, the truth about who started it usually comes out, often from someone who saw.
fact: Twisting the truth to blame a grown-up, like a babysitter, can get them in trouble at work.
fact: An excuse that works twice is still a lie, and the third time it is often seen through.
fact: Small excuses repeated become a habit that people stop believing.
fact: Blaming a friend for something you did can break up a whole group of friends.
fact: Owning your mistake can save a friendship.
fact: Your mess is your job, even if someone else could tidy it.
fact: Making it right with the person who was punished instead of you is the most important part of putting it right.
exercise: Why did Finn let Rory take the blame for the chalk drawing? | He was scared of getting in trouble | He thought Rory had drawn it, not him at all | Rory had asked to take the blame for it | He forgot he had done it by home time
exercise: What did Rory lose because of Finn's silence? | His football practice and his good name | His chalk and his place in the class team | Nothing at all | His best friend forever
exercise: Why is staying silent here a kind of lie? | It lets an innocent person be punished | Silence is always a lie, even when nobody asks | Teachers can hear silence | It isn't a lie at all
exercise: How did Finn put the chalk drawing right? | Told the teacher, said sorry to Rory and scrubbed the wall | Drew on the wall again to show the teacher that it was really him all along | Gave Rory new chalk | Waited until Rory forgot
exercise: A boy says his cousin started the fight, but he did. Who finds out the truth? | The little cousin who saw it tells both mums | Nobody ever, because the aunt's garden has a fence | The police | The cousin's dog
exercise: A boy says his brother pushed first in the car. Dad saw in the mirror. What happens? | Dad knows the truth, and he must say sorry | Only the brother is punished, because he cried | Dad believes him anyway | Nothing, car fights don't count
exercise: A boy tells Mum the babysitter let him stay up late, but he told her he was allowed. Who gets hurt? | The babysitter, who feels bad and could lose the job | Only the boy's little sister, who had to go to bed too | Nobody at all | Only Mum's clock
exercise: Finn said «the bus was late» twice and got away with it. What did he start to believe? | That the excuse always works | That buses are always late | That he should walk faster | That his teacher was kind
exercise: Why did the excuse fail the third time? | His teacher had seen him strolling past the window | The bus company phoned the school to say the bus was early | His friend told on him | He forgot what to say
exercise: Why do small excuses add up to a big problem? | They become a habit, and people stop believing you | Each one is forgotten at once, so they never add up | Teachers like excuses | They make you more punctual
exercise: Finn blamed his best friend for a secret he told. What happened to the group? | Everyone fell out and his best friend stopped speaking to him | They all became closer, because sharing secrets makes friends trust each other | Nothing changed | They blamed someone else
exercise: What saved the friendship? | Finn owning up and saying sorry | Finn keeping quiet about it for a whole month | Finn telling another secret instead | The best friend apologising to Finn
exercise: Dad trips on Lego and hurts his foot. The boy says the Lego is there because of his brother. What is the fair rule? | Your own mess is your job to tidy | Whoever is youngest in the house does the tidying | The person who trips over it tidies | Lego is nobody's job, it's just there
exercise: Two friends are blamed for a broken pot; one did it. The other keeps quiet out of loyalty. What should the one who did it do? | Own up, so his friend isn't punished | Stay quiet, since his friend chose to | Split the blame half and half between them | Blame a third boy who wasn't even there
exercise: Which hurts the person wrongly punished most if it goes on? | Waiting days before owning up | Owning up the same day | Saying sorry in front of others | Helping clean up
exercise: A girl paints on the table and says the pot fell by itself. Paint footprints lead to her seat. What's the lesson? | Things rarely fall by themselves, and the truth shows | Pots often jump off tables when doors bang, so it could be anything | Footprints don't mean anything | Teachers can't follow footprints
test: Rory, quiet: «I missed footie for your chalk, you know». What would put it right? | Tell the teacher it was him and say sorry to Rory | Buy Rory sweets every day for a week and stay quiet about it | Say «thanks mate» and nothing else | Say Rory should've said something
test: «said the bus was late twice n it worked. third time's fine too». What's true? | It can be seen through any time, and it's a habit of lying | It's fine, buses are often late, so nobody can ever prove it's not true | Three is the limit | It only matters if you're very late
test: Babysitter, upset: «He told me he was allowed till ten!» Mum looks at her son. What should he say? | «It's true, I told her that. Sorry to you both» | «She's making it all up because she's cross» | «I don't remember» | «She said I could»
test: Best friend: «Everyone thinks I told your secret. I didn't. YOU did». What saves the friendship? | Admit it to everyone and say sorry | Say she must have told too | Stop talking to everyone | Tell another secret to distract
test: Aunt, firmly: «Little Ollie says YOU threw the first punch». What's the best thing to do? | Tell the truth and say sorry to his cousin and aunt | Say the cousin deserved it for being so annoying all day | Call Ollie a liar | Say nothing
test: Dad, eyes in the mirror: «I saw who pushed first». What is the wise reply? | «It was me. Sorry» to Dad and his brother | «He pushed me first, in my head anyway» | «The car bumped» | «You weren't looking»
test: Kid: «my mate took the blame and didn't mind, so it's fine». What is wrong with that? | He was still punished for nothing; it's still on you to own up | It's his choice to take it, so there's no wrong in it, he's a good mate | It's fine if he doesn't mind | Friends always take blame
test: Teacher: «Interesting, I saw you walk past my window at five to nine». What is the honest answer? | «Sorry, I dawdled. I'll be on time» | «The bus dropped me there, miss, honest» | «That was my twin brother you saw, miss» | «Your clock is wrong, it's always fast»
test: Dad, limping: «Whose Lego is this??» It's the boy's. What's the right answer? | «Mine, sorry Dad. I'll tidy it all» | «It was there already when I got home» | «My brother's» | «You should look down»
test: Which is TRUE about owning up after someone else was punished? | It's late, but it still clears their name and helps | It's useless once they're punished, so there's no point now | It makes things worse for everyone | It only matters if they ask
test: Teacher: «Paint footprints, from the pot to your chair. Fell by itself, did it?» What's best? | Own up and clean the table and floor | Say someone else walked through the mud | Say the pot is haunted | Say nothing
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning silence which permits an innocent person to be punished. | Such silence is a form of deception which deprives the innocent person of his good name | Such silence is a form of loyalty to oneself and is commendable where the punishment is slight | Silence can never constitute a wrong | Such silence is wrong only where the punishment is severe
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a false excuse that has succeeded on two occasions ought not to be repeated. | It remains a falsehood and is liable to be detected, establishing a habit of deceit | It ceases to be effective after two occasions by rule | It becomes truthful upon repetition | It is permissible only upon two occasions
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution to a babysitter of a permission she did not grant? | It may injure the babysitter in respect of her employment and reputation | It is a harmless convenience, since the parents cannot verify the bedtime in question | It is of no consequence to the babysitter | It is permissible where the child is tired
exam: Determine the measure most conducive to the restoration of a friendship damaged by the false accusation of a friend. | A full admission of the truth accompanied by an apology | The accusation of a third person who may have heard the secret | A prolonged period of silence | The disclosure of a further secret
